  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 235 AM EDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nThe stout 600 decameter H5 heat dome will finally begin to weaken \nand shift west for the latter half of the week. The main high \npressure center will move west of the Mississippi by Thursday, with \nmid-level height falls occurring across Florida as and inverted \ntrough begins to push west across the Gulf Stream. There is very \ngood agreement amongst both ensemble and deterministic guidance that \na cut-off upper low will develop on the southeast side of the ridge. \nThis will occur as part of the upper level jet detaches from the \nmain flow over the Great Lakes, creating a Rex Block pattern across \nthe southeast, with deepening low pressure over Florida and \npersistent ridging over the Deep South. This shift in the upper \nlevel pattern will create much more unsettled weather across South \nFlorida, with PoPs climbing above 80% each day through the end of \nthe week. \n\nContinued deep moisture (with PWATs above 2 inches) and increased \nforcing aloft should allow for scattered to numerous storms to \ndevelop across the region. While there is still some spread in the \nguidance, most ensemble QPF clusters are only showing about 1.5 to \n2.5 inches of rainfall for the Thursday through Friday period. These \namounts shouldn't result in anything more than a few isolated urban \nflooding impacts for low lying and poor drainage locations. At the \nsurface, pressure gradients will relax across the region after \nWednesday, knocking down ENE winds back to around ten knots. This \nshould result in slower and more erratic storm motions, with \ndevelopment following outflow and boundary collisions. But with the \nupper low moving overhead, increased forcing may allow for a few \ntraining storms to hit the same area multiple times. As the low \ncrosses into the Gulf over the weekend, there is also an indication \nthat there could be some coastal convergence and pinned coastal \nstorms. While not quite perpendicular to each other, winds at 500 \nand 700 mbs will be out of the SW, while winds at the surface will \nbe out of the E to NE. This may lead to slow moving storms and \nenhanced moisture convergence along the Paradise Coast, which also \nhappens to be where most ensemble QPF clusters show the highest \nrainfall amounts to be (although mostly offshore). \n\nWhile more uncertain, there will also be an isolated threat of \nsevere weather as the disturbance crosses the region. Some models \nare showing that 500 mb temperatures may drop into the -8 to -9 C \nrange, within the 25th percentile for this time of year. This could \nresult in some steeper lapse rates, allowing for some stronger cores \nto develop and for a low chance of hail and strong downburst winds. \nHowever, increased cloud cover may inhibit the amount of diurnal \nheating and instability that accumulates throughout the day, keeping \nthe threat of strong storms more conditional and low confidence. \n\nOverall, highs will remain in the upper 80s to low 90s across SoFlo, \nwith lows in the 70s. Once the upper low ejects out to the west on \nSunday, we should return to our more typical sea-breeze dominated \nwind regime, with scattered afternoon storms.",
